# Web layer

## Forms and redirects

- Every `POST` form includes the CSRF token; CSRF protection stays enabled:

```html
<input type="hidden" th:name="${_csrf.parameterName}" th:value="${_csrf.token}"/>
```

- A `POST` handler ends in `redirect:` and reports its outcome through
  `RedirectAttributes` flash attributes named `message` (success) or `error` (failure).
  Templates render both near the top of the page.
- Any redirect target that came from the request must pass through
  `AppController.safeRedirect`, which keeps only relative in-app paths. Likewise, a URL
  from feed content is only rendered as a link after `AppController.safeHttpUrl`.
  Both are static and unit-tested — extend the tests when you change them.
- A missing entity throws `ArticleService.NotFoundException` so the handler in
  `GlobalExceptionHandler` renders `error` with a 404, instead of returning a blank page.

## Rendering untrusted HTML

`th:utext` is only ever given a string that `HtmlSanitizer` produced. Feed and article
HTML is never interpolated raw, and never passed to `th:utext` straight from the database.
Use `th:text` for everything else.

## Works without JavaScript

The UI is server-rendered and must stay usable with JavaScript disabled: navigation and
actions are links and forms, not click handlers. `static/js/reader.js` is the single
exception — it splits an already-complete document into pages. Anything it adds must
degrade to a normally scrolling page with the same content and links.

## Paging for e-ink

Kindle panels redraw slowly, so lists and articles are laid out a page at a time rather
than scrolled. `reader.js` is driven entirely by `data-reader-*` attributes on the markup
(`data-reader-frame`, `data-reader-prev-url`, `data-reader-next-form`,
`data-reader-key`, …); wire new pages up by emitting those attributes instead of adding
script. Keep the reading area sized to the viewport so one page turn replaces exactly one
screenful.

Paging forward through the article list posts `/items/advance`, which marks the articles
of the current page read. An unread list shrinks by exactly those articles, so the
redirect returns to the same page number rather than the next one. Preserve that
distinction when touching list navigation, and keep the plain *Older articles* link that
moves on without marking anything.

Actions started from the list return to the list: pass the current `listPath` as the
`redirect` parameter so sending an article does not open it.
